Now also need to verify the inspect tile logic in the POINTERUP handler is correct. Let me re-read my current change:

```ts
if (shouldBuild) {
    const tile = pickedTile();
    if (tile) callbacks.onGroundClick?.(tile);
} else if (
    pointerInfo.event.button === 0 &&
    primaryClickStart !== null
) {
    const tile = pickedTile();
    if (tile) callbacks.onInspectTile?.(tile);
}
if (pointerInfo.event.button === 0) primaryClickStart = null;
```

Now with the fix, `primaryClickStart` will be set on any left click. But there's still a subtlety: when `shouldBuild` is true but `primaryClickStart` is null (because it was cleared by drag), both the inspect and build won't fire. That's correct behavior (drag cancels the click).

But when `shouldBuild` is false (no build tool) and `primaryClickStart` is not null, the inspect fires. Good.

Wait, but there's also: when you have a build tool active and click on a house's tile, `shouldBuild` will be true, so it fires `onGroundClick` (which builds on that tile), not `onInspectTile`. That's correct - if you have a tool selected, clicking should build, not inspect.

But there's a timing issue - when `buildTool` transitions from non-null to null (tool is deselected), and you click immediately... Actually, that doesn't matter because each click generates its own pointer events.

Let me verify with typecheck and test:

The user says clicking on a different house in 3D doesn't switch to show that house's details - it just stays on the previously shown house. Let me debug this.

The issue is in `handleInspectTile`. When the house panel is already open showing house #1, and the user clicks on house #2 in the 3D scene, `handleInspectTile` calls `toggleHousePanel(house.id)`.

But `toggleHousePanel` has this logic:
```ts
const toggleHousePanel = (focusHouseId?: number | null) => {
    const nextOpen = focusHouseId !== undefined || !housePanelOpenRef.current;
    ...
}
```

When `focusHouseId` is passed (which it always is from `handleInspectTile`), it always sets `nextOpen = true`. Then:
```ts
setHousePanelOpen(nextOpen);
if (nextOpen) {
    setHousePanelFocusId(focusHouseId ?? null);
    ...
}
```

And in the render:
```tsx
{housePanelOpen && housePanelData && (
    <HouseListPanel
        snapshot={housePanelData}
        onClose={() => toggleHousePanel()}
        focusHouseId={housePanelFocusId}
    />
)}
```

The `HouseListPanel` uses `focusHouseId` as the initial value for `selectedHouseId`:
```ts
const [selectedHouseId, setSelectedHouseId] = useState<number | null>(
    focusHouseId ?? null,
);
```

The problem is: `useState` only uses the initial value on the FIRST render. When `focusHouseId` changes from 1 to 2, the component doesn't re-initialize its state. So it stays showing house #1.

The fix: either use `useEffect` to watch `focusHouseId` and update `selectedHouseId`, or: always unmount and re-mount the panel when `focusHouseId` changes.

The simplest fix: in the HouseListPanel, use a `useEffect` to sync `focusHouseId` to `selectedHouseId`:

```ts
useEffect(() => {
    if (focusHouseId !== undefined && focusHouseId !== null) {
        setSelectedHouseId(focusHouseId);
    }
}, [focusHouseId]);
```

Or simpler: pass a `key` prop to force re-mount when the focus changes. But that would lose the list state.

Better: use the `useEffect` approach.

