Now there's another subtlety: `housePanelFocusId` state change in App.tsx triggers a re-render, but only when the value actually changes. If you click house #1, then click it again, it should still work because `focusHouseId` is the same. But the `useEffect` only fires when `focusHouseId` changes.

And there's yet another issue: when clicking a different house in 3D, the panel is already open with `housePanelOpen = true`. The `toggleHousePanel(house.id)` call sets `nextOpen = true` (unchanged), `setHousePanelFocusId(house.id)` (changed). But `setHousePanelData` is also called but `housePanelData` state is already set. React might batch these state updates and only render once, which is fine.

But wait - does `toggleHousePanel` even re-set `housePanelData` in this case? Let me check:

```ts
const toggleHousePanel = (focusHouseId?: number | null) => {
    const nextOpen = focusHouseId !== undefined || !housePanelOpenRef.current;
    housePanelOpenRef.current = nextOpen;
    setHousePanelOpen(nextOpen);
    if (nextOpen) {
        setHousePanelFocusId(focusHouseId ?? null);
        const snapshot = snapshotRef.current;
        if (snapshot) setHousePanelData(snapshot);
    }
};
```

When `focusHouseId` is passed and panel is already open, `nextOpen = true`, and `setHousePanelData(snapshot)` is called with the same snapshot (unchanged). React should skip the re-render for that state change. Only `setHousePanelFocusId` change triggers a re-render.

But wait - `focusHouseId` is `house.id` (a number), not null. And `housePanelFocusId` is `number | null`. When they're different, it triggers. When they're the same (clicking same house again), it doesn't - which is correct, no need to do anything.

Actually, there's another subtle issue. When the panel is already open showing house #1, and you click house #2 in 3D, the `uiEffect` triggers `setHousePanelFocusId(2)`. This causes a re-render of App, which passes the new `focusHouseId={2}` to `HouseListPanel`. The `useEffect` in `HouseListPanel` catches this and calls `setSelectedHouseId(2)`, switching the detail view.

One more issue: the `handleInspectTile` in App.tsx closes the population panel when a house is clicked:
```ts
populationPanelOpenRef.current = false;
setPopulationPanelOpen(false);
```

This is fine - it just ensures the population panel closes when you click a house.

